Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: DNS Resolution Problems
Symptoms: Cannot resolve IdM server hostnames

Solution:

# Check DNS configuration
cat /etc/resolv.conf

# Test DNS resolution
nslookup idm-primary.lab.local

# Restart DNS service
sudo systemctl restart named-pkcs11
Issue 2: Replication Not Working
Symptoms: Changes don't appear on replica servers

Solution:

# Check replication status
ipa-replica-manage list
ipa-replica-manage list-ruv

# Force replication
ipa-replica-manage force-sync --from idm-primary.lab.local

# Check for replication errors
sudo journalctl -u ipa -f
Issue 3: Authentication Failures
Symptoms: Cannot authenticate with kinit

Solution:

# Check Kerberos configuration
cat /etc/krb5.conf

# Test Kerberos connectivity
telnet idm-primary.lab.local 88

# Clear credential cache
kdestroy
kinit admin
Issue 4: Web UI Not Accessible
Symptoms: Cannot access IdM web interface

Solution:

# Check Apache status
sudo systemctl status httpd

# Check SSL certificates
sudo ipa-server-certinstall --help

# Restart web services
sudo systemctl restart httpd
Verification and Testing Checklist
Use this checklist to verify your IdM topology is working correctly:

Primary Server Verification
 IdM services are running (systemctl status ipa)
 DNS resolution works for all servers
 Web UI is accessible (https://idm-primary.lab.local/ipa/ui/)
 Admin user can authenticate
 Can create and manage users/groups
Replica Server Verification
 IdM services are running on both replicas
 DNS resolution works from replicas
 Web UI is accessible on replicas
 Admin authentication works on replicas
 Directory data is replicated
Replication Verification
 User created on primary appears on replicas
 Group created on primary appears on replicas
 DNS records replicate between servers
 Password changes replicate
 Topology segments are healthy
High Availability Verification
 Services continue when primary is down
 Authentication works through replicas
 DNS resolution continues through replicas
 Changes sync when primary returns
Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully implemented a single-site IdM topology with the following accomplishments:

What You Achieved
Deployed a Complete IdM Infrastructure: You installed and configured a primary IdM server with integrated DNS, providing centralized identity management for your organization.

Implemented High Availability: By deploying two replica servers, you created a resilient infrastructure that can continue operating even if the primary server fails.

Configured DNS Integration: You set up integrated DNS services that automatically manage service discovery records, making client configuration seamless.

Tested Replication and Failover: You verified that directory data, DNS records, and authentication services replicate correctly across all servers and tested failover scenarios.

Established Monitoring Procedures: You created scripts and procedures to monitor replication health and troubleshoot common issues.

Why This Matters
Business Continuity: The redundant topology ensures that authentication and directory services remain available even during server maintenance or failures, minimizing business disruption.

Scalability: This foundation can be expanded to support multiple sites, additional replicas, and thousands of users as your organization grows.

Security: Centralized identity management provides consistent security policies, password requirements, and access controls across your entire infrastructure.

Operational Efficiency: Automated replication and integrated DNS reduce administrative overhead and eliminate single points of failure.

